            <td>12x12</td>
            <td>18</td>
            <td>Experienced players who think in whole networks</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Bigger boards mean more islands, more possible bridges, and more ways to trap yourself with a disconnected final move. If it is your first time playing a hashi puzzle online, <a href="/hashi/">start with an easy 8x8 board</a>, finish it, and only then move up to Medium and Hard.</p>

      <h2>Hashi Tips for Beginners</h2>
      <p>New players ask for the same handful of shortcuts, so here they are:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lock degree-1 islands early.</strong> An island that still needs exactly 1 bridge can take it from any neighbor, so decide its direction before its options shrink.</li>
        <li><strong>Prefer double bridges as anchors.</strong> A double bridge consumes two slots on both islands at once and is almost impossible to reroute later. Locking doubles early keeps the rest of the board flexible.</li>
        <li><strong>Finish corners first.</strong> Corner islands have the fewest directions, so they are the most constrained and the fastest to settle.</li>
        <li><strong>Check connectedness last, but always.</strong> The classic endgame trap is a fully counted island that is stranded. Before you celebrate, make sure every island can still reach the network.</li>
        <li><strong>Let red guide you.</strong> When an island glows red in the GridPaw game, undo your last bridge and recount. The red tint is a prompt, not a penalty.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details>
        <summary>What does Hashiwokakero mean?</summary>
        <p>Hashiwokakero (橋をかけろ) is Japanese for <em>"let's build bridges"</em>. It is the original name of the puzzle, published by Nikoli beginning in 1990, and it is usually shortened to Hashi or translated to Bridges. All three names describe the exact same game.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Is Hashi the same as Bridges?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Hashi is simply a short form of Hashiwokakero, and Bridges is the English name Western publications gave it. Same islands, same numbered clues, same rules, same solution. Only the sticker on the box changes.</p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Is Hashi like Sudoku?</summary>
        <p>No, Hashi is not a Sudoku variant, though both are pure-logic puzzles from the Nikoli tradition. Sudoku fills a grid of digits by elimination, while Hashi builds a connected graph of bridges by planning. In Hashi you never place a number; the island numbers are given and the work is counting connections.</p>
      </details>

